--- !u!114 &293856345
MonoBehaviour:
  m_ObjectHideFlags: 0
  m_CorrespondingSourceObject: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabInstance: {fileID: 0}
  m_PrefabAsset: {fileID: 0}
  m_GameObject: {fileID: 293856343}
  m_Enabled: 1
  m_EditorHideFlags: 0
  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 791b0d3efb21a4a4b9dcec06ebc143f8, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  titleText: {fileID: 1704034428}
  descriptionText: {fileID: 1188270921}
  image: {fileID: 1418679439}
  inputField: {fileID: 436784339}
  parentAnswerObjects: {fileID: 86145367}
  submitButton: {fileID: 1422519293}
  questions:
  - title: 'Part 1/9: Cube Root of 1'
    description: It is important to know how to calculate the cube root of a complex
      number. As all real numbers are a subset of complex numbers, 1 is an element
      of the complex number system. Although straightforward, please find the cube
      root of 1.
    imageTexture: {fileID: 2800000, guid: dc898abed7eb6ff4a89b585bbf9a898e, type: 3}
  - title: 'Part 2/9: Other Cube Roots of 1'
    description: "As we covered in the Fundamental Theorem of algebra, because we
      found the cube root of 1, this means two other complex solutions exist. A standard
      calculator will not output these numbers, as they only factor the principal
      root of the expression. If we look at the expression in a different way, we
      can see where these two other complex solutions exist. By factoring z\xB3 -
      1 = 0 using the difference of cubes method, we discover that a quadratic equation
      (z\xB2+z+1) exists within the expression. Your role now is to solve this equation
      and find the two other roots that exist. <color=yellow>Make sure that you round
      your complex answers to 3 decimal places. </color>"
    imageTexture: {fileID: 2800000, guid: 5d83e88e52f819746abb736edb71c854, type: 3}
  - title: 'Part 3/9: Other Cube Roots of 1'
    description: "As we covered in the Fundamental Theorem of algebra, because we
      found the cube root of 1, this means two other complex solutions exist. A standard
      calculator will not output these numbers, as they only factor the principal
      root of the expression. If we look at the expression in a different way, we
      can see where these two other complex solutions exist. By factoring z\xB3 -
      1 = 0 using the difference of cubes method, we discover that a quadratic equation
      (z\xB2+z+1) exists within the expression. Your role now is to solve this equation
      and find the two other roots that exist. <color=yellow>Make sure that you round
      your complex answers to 3 decimal places. </color>"
    imageTexture: {fileID: 2800000, guid: 5d83e88e52f819746abb736edb71c854, type: 3}
  - title: 'Part 4/9: First Cubic part'
    description: "Now that you understand how finding the cube root works, it\u2019s
      time to find the main components of the cubic equation. The main reason these
      terms are divided by factors of (a) is to reduce the expression to a monic
      polynomial. This means that through this conversion, the leading coefficient
      of the cubed term will be 1. <color=yellow>Make sure that you round your complex
      answers to 3 decimal places. </color>"
    imageTexture: {fileID: 2800000, guid: 4d2cf4b21e98b7f4593e5addaf1cb98f, type: 3}
  - title: 'Part 5/9: Second Cubic part'
    description: The second part also follows the principles of the first part as
      to reduce all these terms such that the equation will become monic. <color=yellow>Make
      sure that you round your complex answers to 3 decimal places. </color>
    imageTexture: {fileID: 2800000, guid: 8c179f798313fda49a16acbc916aec63, type: 3}
  - title: 'Part 6/9: Third Cubic part'
    description: The goal of this teaching is to break down the steps necessary to
      complete the cubic equation. In this situation, these parts have exponential
      properties associated with them. In addition, the square root of the sum of
      these expressions is required to progressed. <b>When it comes to dealing with
      series of complex operations, you are allowed to break them down into smaller
      pieces. </b> Find the squared and cubed terms first through using your calculator.
      When finding the square root in this context, we are only concerned about the
      principal root for now. This refers to the positive complex number outputted
      from the equation. <color=yellow>Make sure that you round your complex answers
      to 3 decimal places. </color>
    imageTexture: {fileID: 2800000, guid: ce90430c1e7ab384bae627a4a4e12671, type: 3}
  - title: 'Part 7/9: Third Cubic part'
    description: The goal of this teaching is to break down the steps necessary to
      complete the cubic equation. In this situation, these parts have exponential
      properties associated with them. In addition, the square root of the sum of
      these expressions is required to progressed. <b>When it comes to dealing with
      series of complex operations, you are allowed to break them down into smaller
      pieces. </b> Find the squared and cubed terms first through using your calculator.
      When finding the square root in this context, we are only concerned about the
      principal root for now. This refers to the positive complex number outputted
      from the equation. <color=yellow>Make sure that you round your complex answers
      to 3 decimal places. </color>
    imageTexture: {fileID: 2800000, guid: 105be6083c189604fb0f15ba563eebfc, type: 3}
  - title: 'Part 8/9: Third Cubic part'
    description: The goal of this teaching is to break down the steps necessary to
      complete the cubic equation. In this situation, these parts have exponential
      properties associated with them. In addition, the square root of the sum of
      these expressions is required to progressed. <b>When it comes to dealing with
      series of complex operations, you are allowed to break them down into smaller
      pieces. </b> Find the squared and cubed terms first through using your calculator.
      When finding the square root in this context, we are only concerned about the
      principal root for now. This refers to the positive complex number outputted
      from the equation. <color=yellow>Make sure that you round your complex answers
      to 3 decimal places. </color>
    imageTexture: {fileID: 2800000, guid: dc6fa6290821d7b49aeb7bc2c37cbda0, type: 3}
  - title: 'Part 9/9: Third Cubic part'
    description: The goal of this teaching is to break down the steps necessary to
      complete the cubic equation. In this situation, these parts have exponential
      properties associated with them. In addition, the square root of the sum of
      these expressions is required to progressed. <b>When it comes to dealing with
      series of complex operations, you are allowed to break them down into smaller
      pieces. </b> Find the squared and cubed terms first through using your calculator.
      When finding the square root in this context, we are only concerned about the
      principal root for now. This refers to the positive complex number outputted
      from the equation. <color=yellow>Make sure that you round your complex answers
      to 3 decimal places. </color>
    imageTexture: {fileID: 2800000, guid: 4a8b8e14e6a0f61468f21ab333788660, type: 3}
  - title: First Root
    description: "This is the final stretch! All we need to do is find the final
      roots of the equation. As you can see, \u03C9 is used in finding the roots
      of the equation. Just like finding the cube root of 1, the cube root of any
      complex number will have 3 distinct roots. In the previous segments, we only
      considered the principal roots for each cube root. However, within the roots
      for the equation, we will need all the roots for the equations. Complex numbers
      are necessary to find the roots for the cubic equation, as the cubic roots
      of unity (the roots we solved for before with z\xB3 - 1 = 0) are necessary
      to find the roots of the equation. <color=yellow>Make sure that you round your
      answer to the nearest whole number.</color>"
    imageTexture: {fileID: 2800000, guid: 106254b66a8a3da4e9ce3b26bedba41a, type: 3}
  - title: Second Root
    description: "This is the final stretch! All we need to do is find the final
      roots of the equation. As you can see, \u03C9 is used in finding the roots
      of the equation. Just like finding the cube root of 1, the cube root of any
      complex number will have 3 distinct roots. In the previous segments, we only
      considered the principal roots for each cube root. However, within the roots
      for the equation, we will need all the roots for the equations. Complex numbers
      are necessary to find the roots for the cubic equation, as the cubic roots
      of unity (the roots we solved for before with z\xB3 - 1 = 0) are necessary
      to find the roots of the equation. <color=yellow>Make sure that you round your
      answer to the nearest whole number.</color>"
    imageTexture: {fileID: 2800000, guid: 6cbd2c41b635f80489c17d849c8d4255, type: 3}
  - title: Third Root
    description: "This is the final stretch! All we need to do is find the final
      roots of the equation. As you can see, \u03C9 is used in finding the roots
      of the equation. Just like finding the cube root of 1, the cube root of any
      complex number will have 3 distinct roots. In the previous segments, we only
      considered the principal roots for each cube root. However, within the roots
